Service with a signal:


- The core idea is to keep a piece of state inside a service and expose only a read-only view to
    consumers. 🔥Components can read the state in templates and react to it, while only the service is
    allowed to update it.

    Let’s define a UserService with a read-only currentUser signal:

        @Service()
        export class UserService {
            private readonly user = signal<UserModel | undefined>(undefined);
            readonly currentUser = this.user.asReadonly();
    
    This is just a tiny example. More complex use cases might require several signals, observables, and
    more logic, but this basic principle (storing state in a service in order to share it reactively
    between several components) is an extremely common pattern. The service can also be provided by a
    component instead of in root when the state is local to that component and its descendants.
